		<item>
		<title>Design Questions in the Software Liability Debate</title>
		<link>https://dfrlab.org/2024/01/16/design-questions-in-the-software-liability-debate/</link>
		<pubDate>Tue, 16 Jan 2024 10:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Cyber Statecraft Initiative]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Cybersecurity]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Cybersecurity Standards and Requirements]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Report]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Maia Hamin]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Trey Herr]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Sara Ann Brackett]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://dfrlab.org/?p=230043713</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Software liability—resurgent in the policy debate since its mention in the 2023 US National Cybersecurity Strategy—describes varied potential structures to create legal accountability for vendors of insecure software. This report identifies key design questions for such regimes and tracks their discussion through the decades-long history of the debate. </p>
<p>The post <a href="https://dfrlab.org/2024/01/16/design-questions-in-the-software-liability-debate/">Design Questions in the Software Liability Debate</a> appeared first on <a href="https://dfrlab.org">DFRLab</a>.</p>
]]></description>
					
		
		
